.SH Specializations

The standard specifies that std::atomic_ref has following specializations:

template<>                                  \fB(1)\fP \fI(since C++20)\fP
struct atomic_ref</*integral-type*/>;
template<>                                  \fB(2)\fP \fI(since C++20)\fP
struct atomic_ref</*floating-point-type*/>;
template< class /*pointer-type*/ >

requires /* see below */                    \fB(3)\fP \fI(since C++20)\fP

struct atomic_ref</*pointer-type*/>;

1) /*integral-type*/ denotes a possibly cv-qualified integral type other than cv bool.
2) /*floating-point-type*/ denotes a possibly cv-qualified floating-point type.
3) The partial specialization is provided for /*pointer-type*/ types that are possibly
cv-qualified pointer-to-object types.

.SH Notes

Implementations may merge the specified specializations. E.g. MSVC STL merges all of them
into the primary template.

When T is cv void or a function type, std::atomic_ref<T*> (i.e. std::atomic_ref<void*>,
std::atomic_ref<int(*)()> etc.) does not have difference_type or any operation requiring
pointer arithmetic
or relational comparison
(since C++26).

